WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.560 --> 00:00:03.140
One last thing we're going to look at

2
00:00:03.140 --> 00:00:05.620
is how to attach something to our character.

3
00:00:05.620 --> 00:00:08.300
Because so often in games and cinematics,

4
00:00:08.300 --> 00:00:10.039
a character might be wearing something

5
00:00:10.039 --> 00:00:12.560
or picking something up, equipping something.

6
00:00:12.560 --> 00:00:14.859
And this is essentially just a great thing

7
00:00:14.859 --> 00:00:16.739
to know how to do.

8
00:00:16.739 --> 00:00:20.140
So in the engine, we do this using sockets.

9
00:00:20.140 --> 00:00:23.019
Now a socket is something that we add to the skeleton

10
00:00:23.019 --> 00:00:24.539
and it's parented to a bone

11
00:00:24.539 --> 00:00:27.460
and it just follows that bone wherever it goes.

12
00:00:27.460 --> 00:00:28.800
We'll then add the asset

13
00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:31.199
that we want to attach into our sequence.

14
00:00:31.199 --> 00:00:33.380
We'll reference the socket inside the sequence

15
00:00:33.380 --> 00:00:35.570
to attach the two together.

16
00:00:35.570 --> 00:00:37.250
Now in the sequence, we're going to use

17
00:00:37.250 --> 00:00:39.810
an attach track to do this attachment.

18
00:00:39.810 --> 00:00:43.490
But you can also use things like blueprint logic

19
00:00:43.490 --> 00:00:47.060
and events in the timeline to trigger those attachments.

20
00:00:47.060 --> 00:00:48.939
So let's add a socket to our skeleton first

21
00:00:48.939 --> 00:00:51.259
and then we can set that up in the sequence.

22
00:00:51.259 --> 00:00:53.539
So to find a skeleton for a character,

23
00:00:53.539 --> 00:00:55.039
I'm going to select the character

24
00:00:55.039 --> 00:00:58.179
that we have in the level, press Control B

25
00:00:58.179 --> 00:01:01.770
and that'll browse to the asset in your content browser.

26
00:01:02.409 --> 00:01:03.719
If I was to open that up,

27
00:01:03.719 --> 00:01:06.680
we can then use the animation sub-editor icon

28
00:01:06.680 --> 00:01:08.079
at the top here to find the skeleton

29
00:01:08.079 --> 00:01:10.099
that's connected to this character.

30
00:01:10.099 --> 00:01:12.120
So if I select that,

31
00:01:12.120 --> 00:01:14.140
this is now a skeleton that we're using

32
00:01:14.140 --> 00:01:15.159
and we've got a skeleton tree

33
00:01:15.159 --> 00:01:16.719
in the left hand side here.

34
00:01:16.719 --> 00:01:19.819
So I'm going to select probably spine four

35
00:01:19.819 --> 00:01:23.049
or spine five here, let's go with spine five.

36
00:01:23.049 --> 00:01:26.659
I'll right click this and I will add a socket.

37
00:01:26.659 --> 00:01:30.280
And if we select the socket here, oops,

38
00:01:30.280 --> 00:01:34.040
you can see if I change our preview settings,

39
00:01:34.040 --> 00:01:37.150
so character bones and tick sockets here,

40
00:01:37.150 --> 00:01:38.390
you'll be able to see any sockets

41
00:01:38.390 --> 00:01:40.430
that we have on our character.

42
00:01:40.430 --> 00:01:42.230
So you can see we have one here,

43
00:01:42.230 --> 00:01:44.870
there'll be like some sort of item attachment socket

44
00:01:44.870 --> 00:01:48.239
and we've got the spine of five socket.

45
00:01:48.239 --> 00:01:52.069
So if I right click and rename our socket,

46
00:01:52.069 --> 00:01:55.209
we'll just call this backpack underscore socket

47
00:01:55.209 --> 00:01:56.730
because we're going to be attaching a backpack

48
00:01:56.730 --> 00:01:58.760
to our character.

49
00:01:58.760 --> 00:02:00.840
Now, if we were to go back to our skeletal mesh

50
00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:02.719
and have a look in our skeleton tree,

51
00:02:02.719 --> 00:02:05.620
we should also see that socket there.

52
00:02:05.620 --> 00:02:14.050
If I save this, we don't, which is interesting.

53
00:02:14.050 --> 00:02:18.789
How about I close it and I open it again.

54
00:02:18.789 --> 00:02:20.830
There we go, so it's just refreshed now.

55
00:02:20.830 --> 00:02:23.500
So we can see our backpack socket here

56
00:02:23.500 --> 00:02:25.530
connected to our spine of five.

57
00:02:25.530 --> 00:02:27.129
So coming back to our sequence here,

58
00:02:27.129 --> 00:02:28.789
we can now add our backpack

59
00:02:28.789 --> 00:02:30.569
that we want to attach to the character

60
00:02:30.569 --> 00:02:33.199
and we can set up the attach track.

61
00:02:33.199 --> 00:02:34.740
I jump over to the content browser

62
00:02:34.740 --> 00:02:37.800
and if I just search for backpack

63
00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:41.710
and then click and hold the skeletal mesh version,

64
00:02:41.710 --> 00:02:43.069
hover over the sequencer tab

65
00:02:43.069 --> 00:02:44.990
and drop it into our sequencer window here,

66
00:02:44.990 --> 00:02:46.349
that'll bring it in.

67
00:02:46.349 --> 00:02:49.710
If I just minimize money to make this a bit clearer.

68
00:02:49.710 --> 00:02:51.500
To add an attach track,

69
00:02:51.500 --> 00:02:53.979
just over the items that you want to attach,

70
00:02:53.979 --> 00:02:56.830
hit the plus on the right hand side here,

71
00:02:56.830 --> 00:02:59.189
hover over attach and choose the asset

72
00:02:59.189 --> 00:03:00.969
that you want to attach it to.

73
00:03:00.969 --> 00:03:02.789
So if the asset that you want to attach it to

74
00:03:02.789 --> 00:03:04.650
is inside your sequence already,

75
00:03:04.650 --> 00:03:07.409
you can choose it from this existing binding heading.

76
00:03:07.409 --> 00:03:08.689
But if it's something that's in the level

77
00:03:08.689 --> 00:03:09.969
that's not in the sequence,

78
00:03:09.969 --> 00:03:11.090
you could go to new binding

79
00:03:11.090 --> 00:03:13.270
and choose that from your outline here.

80
00:03:13.270 --> 00:03:14.729
But we want to attach it to money,

81
00:03:14.729 --> 00:03:17.900
so we'll just click that.

82
00:03:17.900 --> 00:03:18.740
And we can see here,

83
00:03:18.740 --> 00:03:20.360
we've got a handful of sockets

84
00:03:20.360 --> 00:03:21.780
and all the joints of our character

85
00:03:21.780 --> 00:03:24.189
that we're able to attach this to.

86
00:03:24.189 --> 00:03:25.789
But we're just gonna choose backpack socket

87
00:03:25.789 --> 00:03:29.729
because that's the one we set up to attach.

88
00:03:29.729 --> 00:03:31.810
Now, you can see here

89
00:03:31.810 --> 00:03:34.449
that it has attached to our character

90
00:03:34.449 --> 00:03:36.569
and it's jumped up onto that socket.

91
00:03:36.569 --> 00:03:38.250
So as we scrub through and play,

92
00:03:38.250 --> 00:03:40.370
it's following our character around.

93
00:03:40.370 --> 00:03:43.289
But it doesn't look quite right.

94
00:03:43.289 --> 00:03:46.490
So it's halfway inside our character.

95
00:03:46.490 --> 00:03:48.930
Now we could correct this one of two ways.

96
00:03:48.930 --> 00:03:50.830
We could select the backpack

97
00:03:50.830 --> 00:03:53.550
and then come to frame zero here,

98
00:03:53.550 --> 00:03:55.189
set a key on the transform track

99
00:03:55.189 --> 00:03:57.509
and just move it out

100
00:03:57.509 --> 00:04:02.740
and reposition it to however we want the asset to look.

101
00:04:02.740 --> 00:04:04.639
But if this is something you're doing,

102
00:04:04.639 --> 00:04:06.479
you know, several times,

103
00:04:06.479 --> 00:04:07.819
then you don't really want

104
00:04:07.819 --> 00:04:09.159
to have to do this step every time.

105
00:04:09.159 --> 00:04:11.379
Or if you're doing it in blueprints with an event,

106
00:04:11.379 --> 00:04:14.060
you don't want to have to set up any extra logic there.

107
00:04:14.060 --> 00:04:16.939
So we can actually change the default location

108
00:04:16.939 --> 00:04:21.379
of our backpack socket to account for this.

109
00:04:21.379 --> 00:04:27.600
So if we jump back to our skeleton socket here,

110
00:04:27.699 --> 00:04:30.199
we can see that we've got our socket selected

111
00:04:30.199 --> 00:04:32.560
and we can add a preview mesh to this.

112
00:04:32.560 --> 00:04:35.279
So we can see what a mesh might look like

113
00:04:35.279 --> 00:04:37.660
when it gets attached to the socket.

114
00:04:37.660 --> 00:04:39.319
So if I right click the socket

115
00:04:39.319 --> 00:04:41.720
and choose add preview asset,

116
00:04:41.720 --> 00:04:44.860
I can then search for backpack

117
00:04:44.860 --> 00:04:46.779
and that'll allow us to add it to,

118
00:04:48.490 --> 00:04:50.410
as a preview mesh to see what it looks like

119
00:04:50.410 --> 00:04:51.910
when it's attaching.

120
00:04:51.910 --> 00:04:54.589
Now, because we're using SKM Manny

121
00:04:54.589 --> 00:04:56.990
as our preview inside the sequence,

122
00:04:56.990 --> 00:05:00.029
I'm actually gonna change our preview mesh here

123
00:05:00.029 --> 00:05:01.230
to match SKM Manny

124
00:05:01.269 --> 00:05:06.180
because his mesh is quite different from Quinn's mesh.

125
00:05:06.180 --> 00:05:07.139
And we want to know that we're working

126
00:05:07.139 --> 00:05:09.019
with the right mesh here.

127
00:05:09.019 --> 00:05:12.540
So if I select the backpack socket

128
00:05:12.540 --> 00:05:15.339
and just bring that out and reposition it

129
00:05:15.339 --> 00:05:18.350
to where we think the backpack looks good,

130
00:05:18.350 --> 00:05:20.839
I think that'll probably work for now.

131
00:05:20.839 --> 00:05:24.050
Straighten up a bit more.

132
00:05:24.050 --> 00:05:25.430
Once we come back out to,

133
00:05:25.430 --> 00:05:26.910
if I save that actually first,

134
00:05:26.910 --> 00:05:29.759
once we come back out to our sequence,

135
00:05:29.759 --> 00:05:33.660
we can see now that we kind of have a double offset

136
00:05:33.660 --> 00:05:35.259
because we have this transform track

137
00:05:35.259 --> 00:05:36.660
working at the same time.

138
00:05:36.660 --> 00:05:38.339
But we can see that there's definitely been a change

139
00:05:38.339 --> 00:05:41.329
and has been an update to the location of this backpack.

140
00:05:41.329 --> 00:05:45.680
So with the backpack selected

141
00:05:45.680 --> 00:05:46.740
and the anim details here,

142
00:05:46.740 --> 00:05:50.300
I'm just gonna hit this arrow, whoops,

143
00:05:50.300 --> 00:05:52.839
to reset the property to the default values,

144
00:05:52.839 --> 00:05:55.269
which is just zero.

145
00:05:55.269 --> 00:05:56.189
And as you can see,

146
00:05:56.189 --> 00:05:59.129
it will snap back to the location

147
00:05:59.129 --> 00:06:01.629
that the socket's now in by default.

148
00:06:01.629 --> 00:06:04.709
So if I set a key just where we are now

149
00:06:04.709 --> 00:06:10.819
and delete that original key and drag this back to zero,

150
00:06:10.819 --> 00:06:13.420
you can see that when a character moves,

151
00:06:13.420 --> 00:06:15.500
our backpack is now attached to them

152
00:06:17.110 --> 00:06:18.490
in the correct location.

153
00:06:18.490 --> 00:06:20.750
And anytime that we attach this backpack,

154
00:06:20.750 --> 00:06:24.750
whether it's in blueprint or whether it's in sequencer,

155
00:06:24.750 --> 00:06:26.750
we know confidently that it's gonna snap to the right

156
00:06:26.750 --> 00:06:30.310
place without us having to add any more transform offsets

157
00:06:30.310 --> 00:06:32.149
to that with like a transform track

158
00:06:32.149 --> 00:06:34.949
or any transform values that might need to be added

159
00:06:37.779 --> 00:06:41.129
after the attachment.

160
00:06:41.129 --> 00:06:42.410
So there it is.

161
00:06:42.410 --> 00:06:44.910
We've just seen how to create a new animation

162
00:06:44.910 --> 00:06:47.660
using Control Rig inside Sequencer.

163
00:06:47.660 --> 00:06:50.139
Also how to blend existing animations together

164
00:06:50.139 --> 00:06:51.720
to create something new

165
00:06:51.720 --> 00:06:54.879
and how to then save that animation asset

166
00:06:54.879 --> 00:06:57.259
to use elsewhere in the project.

167
00:06:57.259 --> 00:06:59.300
And also how you can then animate on top

168
00:06:59.300 --> 00:07:02.180
of a preexisting animation non-destructively

169
00:07:02.180 --> 00:07:04.769
using a layered control rig.

170
00:07:04.810 --> 00:07:07.129
There's really so much more to learn about control rig

171
00:07:07.129 --> 00:07:09.050
and how animations work in engine.

172
00:07:09.050 --> 00:07:10.490
And we have several different courses

173
00:07:10.490 --> 00:07:11.529
that go into great detail

174
00:07:11.529 --> 00:07:13.569
about how to create your own control rig

175
00:07:13.569 --> 00:07:15.889
and how to utilize all of the other animation tools

176
00:07:15.889 --> 00:07:16.930
in the engine.

177
00:07:16.930 --> 00:07:18.889
So if there's more you want to learn about control rig

178
00:07:18.889 --> 00:07:20.589
and how to animate in the engine,

179
00:07:20.589 --> 00:07:25.500
you should definitely go and check those out.

180
00:07:25.500 --> 00:07:27.139
I just wanted to share this last slide

181
00:07:27.139 --> 00:07:28.980
that has a couple of links that goes into

182
00:07:28.980 --> 00:07:31.899
a bit more detail about root motion in the engine,

183
00:07:31.899 --> 00:07:34.180
as well as a link for more detailed breakdown

184
00:07:34.180 --> 00:07:36.300
about animation blueprints

185
00:07:36.300 --> 00:07:38.100
and a link for any Blender add-ons

186
00:07:38.100 --> 00:07:42.180
that exist for the Unreal Engine.

187
00:07:42.180 --> 00:07:43.459
And just to wrap up,

188
00:07:43.459 --> 00:07:45.540
we've learned about the different types of animations

189
00:07:45.540 --> 00:07:46.500
in the engine.

190
00:07:46.500 --> 00:07:49.019
And we now know how to import and export our characters

191
00:07:49.019 --> 00:07:51.519
and animations to and from the engine.

192
00:07:51.519 --> 00:07:53.120
We have an understanding of the differences

193
00:07:53.120 --> 00:07:55.560
between skeletons and skeletal meshes,

194
00:07:55.560 --> 00:07:56.839
and then a brief overview

195
00:07:56.839 --> 00:07:58.639
of the different animation subeditors

196
00:07:58.639 --> 00:08:00.579
and what you could use those for.

197
00:08:00.579 --> 00:08:03.720
Then we actually created our own animation from scratch,

198
00:08:03.720 --> 00:08:06.300
as well as learning how to blend animations together

199
00:08:06.300 --> 00:08:07.779
to create new animations

200
00:08:08.420 --> 00:08:10.420
and how to attach these props to your characters

201
00:08:10.420 --> 00:08:12.500
using sockets.

202
00:08:12.500 --> 00:08:14.180
So thank you very much for listening

203
00:08:14.180 --> 00:08:16.300
and remember to check out any of our other courses

204
00:08:16.300 --> 00:08:18.019
if you want to continue developing

205
00:08:18.019 --> 00:08:20.620
and best of luck with your animations in the future.
